<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Český Krumlov</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Krumau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Český Krumlov, in German Krumau, is a town in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, set in tight bends of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vltava">Vltava</a>. Its castle, one of the largest in the country, was the seat of the Rosenberg family, then of the Eggenbergs, and from 1719 of the princes of Schwarzenberg, who kept it until 1947. Its well-preserved old town is a World Heritage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cesky-krumlov.jpg" alt="Český Krumlov" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DominikCK1999 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blick_auf_das_Stadtzentrum_von_Krumau_(2026).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Český Krumlov lies on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vltava">Vltava</a> in the Bohemian Forest Foothills, about 20 kilometres south-west of České Budějovice and 134 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. The area was settled from the Older Stone Age, with mass settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, Celtic settlements about 400 BCE and the first Slavic settlement from the 6th century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The castle was founded shortly before 1250 by a local branch of the noble Vítkovci family, and the town was first mentioned in a deed of 1253 as Chrumbenowe. It grew in two stages: Latrán, built spontaneously below the castle by people connected with its administration, and a newly founded Old Town. Both Czech and German nationalities were present from the start, and a Jewish community is documented from 1334.</p><p>When the Vítkovci line died out in 1302, King Wenceslaus II ceded the estate to the Rosenberg family, who made it their main residence. Peter I of Rosenberg had the upper castle built in the early 14th century, and the town reached its highest prosperity in the 15th century under Oldřich II of Rosenberg. Gold found near the town in the late 15th century brought German miners, shifting the ethnic balance further.</p><p>In 1555 William of Rosenberg joined Latrán and the Old Town into one, and later rebuilt the castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style. Peter Vok of Rosenberg sold Krumlov in 1602 to Emperor Rudolf II, who gave it to his illegitimate son Julius d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> in 1620 Emperor Ferdinand II gave it to the Eggenberg family, and it became the seat of the Duchy of Krumlov. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-schwarzenberg">Schwarzenberg family</a> held the castle from 1719 to 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1910 the town had 8,662 inhabitants, 85 per cent of them German and 15 per cent Czech. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1919">Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye</a> in 1919, was annexed by Nazi Germany under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> in 1938, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> its German majority was expelled. The historic centre fell into disrepair under communist rule and has been largely restored since 1989. The castle complex, about 7 hectares with forty buildings around five courts, is one of the largest in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the town and castle have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1992. The town has about 13,000 inhabitants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Český Krumlov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Franconian lands were small, but the family's real weight lay in service to the Habsburgs and in vast estates acquired in Bohemia, including Frauenberg (Hluboká), bought in 1661, and <b>Krumau</b> (Český Krumlov), inherited from the Eggenberg family in 1719.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-schwarzenberg">Principality of Schwarzenberg</a></li></ul></section>
